<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pontic–Caspian steppe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pontic-Caspian steppe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pontic–Caspian steppe is the vast grassland stretching from the northern shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. It was home to successive nomadic peoples and empires. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> emerged there in the mid-seventh century following the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe.jpg" alt="Pontic–Caspian steppe" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: YegorGeologist · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9E%D0%B1%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B8_%D0%90%D0%B7%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE-%D0%A1%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%88%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B0_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%91%D0%B8%D1%80%D1%8E%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The steppe stretches from the northern shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, the Pontus Euxinus of antiquity, to the northern area around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, where it ends at the Ural-Caspian narrowing and joins the Kazakh Steppe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> as part of the larger Eurasian Steppe. It covers about 994,000 km2 across north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>, southern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> and the Lower Volga region. The East European forest steppe bounds it on the north, and the drier Caspian lowland desert lies between it and the Caspian Sea. Its flora and fauna are distinctive, and the Ponto-Caspian seas are the remains of the Turgai Sea, an extension of the Paratethys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area corresponds to the Cimmeria, Scythia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatia">Sarmatia</a> of classical antiquity. Across several millennia numerous tribes of nomadic horsemen used the steppe, and many went on to conquer lands in the settled regions of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, West Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. Genetic research has identified the region as the most probable place where horses were first domesticated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis, the most prevalent theory in Indo-European studies, places the homeland of the speakers of Proto-Indo-European on the steppe.</p><p>A sequence of prehistoric cultures is listed for the region, among them the Khvalynsk culture (5000-3500 BCE), the Sredny Stog culture (4500-3500 BCE), the Yamnaya or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> cultures (3500-2300 BCE), the Catacomb culture (3000-2200 BCE) and the Srubna culture (1600-1200 BCE). Peoples and states with origins on the steppe include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and Sarmatians, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, Alans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> of the 11th to 13th centuries and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> of the 13th to 15th. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a>, the Crimean Khanate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> belong to its later history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pontic–Caspian steppe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Khazar Khaganate emerged in the mid-seventh century on the <b>Pontic-Caspian steppe</b> after the Western Turkic Khaganate broke up.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Pontic-Caspian steppe</b> these migrants formed the tribal confederations of the Chernogorovka-Novocherkassk complex, which drew on the native Bilozerka culture, on the Altai, Aržan and Karasuk cultures of Central Asia and Siberia, and on the Kuban culture of Ciscaucasia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1961 Marija Gimbutas argued that the people of the Kurgan culture, the Yamnaya and their forerunners of the <b>Pontic–Caspian steppe</b>, most likely speakers of Proto-Indo-European, destroyed it in a series of invasions; frequent evidence of violent death in Verteba Cave has been cited in support.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Gimbutas argued that the decline was followed by an invasion of horse-riding Proto-Indo-European tribes from the <b>Pontic–Caspian steppe</b>, a view since criticised as “New Age sentiment” and prevalent until the 1990s, when evidence of Neolithic massacres and fortified settlements emerged; the debate over the culture’s end, like its…</blockquote><a href="/culture/vinca-culture">Vinča culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Yamnaya culture, also called the Pit Grave or Ochre Grave culture, is the name archaeologists give to a late Copper Age to early Bronze Age culture of the <b>Pontic–Caspian steppe</b>, between the Southern Bug, Dniester and Ural rivers, dated to about 3300–2600 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yamnaya-culture">Yamnaya culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Yamnaya culture of the <b>Pontic–Caspian steppe</b>, c. 3300–2600 BCE, is likewise an archaeologists' label.</blockquote><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State</a></li></ul></section>
